1
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,000
Hold it right there, Polly!

2
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:54,560
You're not getting away this time!

3
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:56,557
I'm telling you, I'll get you the money!

4
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,497
You just have to wait a little longer!

5
00:01:58,580 --> 00:01:59,457
Is that...

6
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:00,620
Yes, it's Polly.

7
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,597
The debt collectors are after him again.

8
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:03,980
Give us the money!

9
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:07,337
For the last time, I can't give
you money if I don't have it!

10
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:09,880
Stop making excuses and pay up!

11
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,557
You're gonna chase me all the
way to my grave, aren't you?

12
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:15,522
Yaggaras, and just in time, too.

13
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:19,460
Rope, action!

14
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:26,257
Hey, that's one of the shipwrights
from Galley-La, ain't it?

15
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:29,397
Sorry, fellas, but I gotta
borrow those yaggaras now!

16
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,833
Time for my round turn!

17
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,170
Awwww!

18
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:56,780
You punk! Just wait!

19
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:58,137
We'll get you next time!

20
00:02:58,220 --> 00:02:59,217
So long, gentlemen!

21
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,997
It was a wonderful race, as always!

22
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:03,962
Take care, and maybe you
can try again next week!

23
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:05,350
Goddammit!

24
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:09,814
I gotta admit, for Jagera, you sure
have a good sense of dramatic timing.

25
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:14,980
That was close.

26
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,217
Those shipwrights of yours have
got some pretty fancy moves.

27
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,130
That they do.

28
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,460
Hey, buddy, thanks for the help!

29
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:24,657
I was starting to think we'd
never see our money again!

30
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:25,570
Money?

31
00:03:25,653 --> 00:03:27,640
Oh, it must mean this.

32
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:30,880
Hello! No!

33
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:35,380
You gotta be kidding me.

34
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:36,760
Hey, come back here!

35
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:38,517
I'll take care of this.

36
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:40,129
Jerk's stealing our stuff.

37
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,671
I'll get your money back.

38
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:46,250
Huh?

39
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:47,163
Wait, a pigeon?

40
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:51,477
Okay, let me go, let me go.

41
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:52,390
Take it easy.

42
00:03:52,473 --> 00:03:53,303
Jeez.

43
00:03:53,386 --> 00:03:54,297
I'm not gonna run away.

44
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:55,210
You got me.

45
00:03:55,293 --> 00:03:56,278
You win, all right?

46
00:03:56,920 --> 00:03:59,540
You can stop pitching my ear now. Ah!

47
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:02,777
I was lucky enough to find
that money and you screw it up.

48
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:04,297
Better watch your back, Lucci.

49
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,237
I'll remember this.

50
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,857
Don't pay back your debt with
someone else's money, idiot!

51
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,020
Have you no shame?

52
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,577
Hey, I happened upon that
money fair and square!

53
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:13,937
Stealing from thieves is still stealing!

54
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,520
I didn't steal anything.

55
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:17,917
I was just borrowing
the Yagata, that's all.

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,412
Looks like they're back.

57
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:20,690
That's great.

58
00:04:20,773 --> 00:04:23,177
I'm glad things worked out for you, Usopp.

59
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:24,400
Worked out for me?

60
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:27,257
That 200 million was for
ship repairs, remember?

61
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:28,360
That's right!

62
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,217
And all you did was sit there and stare
like a fool while I nearly got carried away!

63
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:35,006
I know, but the physician
said he'd handle it.

64
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:37,020
Well, that was embarrassing.

65
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:40,657
Rest assured, we will deal with
our employee appropriately.

66
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:43,657
Allow us to handle this so
there's no bad blood between you.

67
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:45,837
Although Polly's behavior
was reprehensible,

68
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:48,617
it's fortunate that he saved your
money from the Franky family.

69
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:50,577
Well, I guess you could
look at it that way.

70
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:52,040
No harm done, right?

71
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:56,157
So, who is this Franky family?

72
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:59,157
Do they rob from everyone
they run into or just us?

73
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:02,299
Well, it depends, but
normally they dismantle ships.

74
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,637
You mean they're workmen?

75
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:07,517
I don't know about you, but I'd never
hire a team that looks like that.

76
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:08,430
Indeed.

77
00:05:08,513 --> 00:05:10,077
But they're not just ship dismantlers.

78
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:11,517
They're also bounty hunters.

79
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:15,157
Every time they find out there are pirates
in town, they cause quite a ruckus.

80
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,817
To put it simply, they're
just a public nuisance.

81
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:20,597
Not only do they collect bounties
on the pirates they defeat,

82
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,092
they also take possession of their ships.

83
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:26,897
Afterwards, they dismantle them and sell
whatever parts they can on the open market.

84
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:30,017
It may seem underhanded, but
for them, it's good business.

85
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:31,437
What a bunch of jerkwads!

86
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:34,277
It's like they're trying to
dismantle the whole pirate lifestyle!

87
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:37,237
Well, if they catch you, they
will certainly bleed you dry.

88
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:38,150
I don't know.

89
00:05:38,233 --> 00:05:40,117
Those guys look too weak
to do anything to us.

90
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:42,082
The men you saw were underlings.

91
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:45,997
Franky, the leader of the group,
is not nearly as harmless.

92
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:48,727
If I were you, I would
avoid him at any cost.

93
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:53,997
Here you go iceberg, do
with him as you will.

94
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:55,457
I'm here, you can let go.

95
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,637
Excellent work as always, Lucci!

96
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:00,957
Truly, I can't apologize enough
for the actions of this elf.

97
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:02,880
It is talking.

98
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:08,040
Tell the nice people
that you're sorry, elf.

99
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:12,237
That pigeon really has it out
for that dude, doesn't he?

100
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,357
So the little bird talks but
the guy in the hat doesn't?

101
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:20,100
Anyway, I'm just glad we got our money
back Oh, so these belong to you, huh?

102
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,300
Good thing I was around.

103
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:23,820
Yeah, thanks a lot.

104
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,240
If you want to thank me, give me 10%.

105
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:30,640
Again, I'm quite sorry.

106
00:06:31,880 --> 00:06:34,377
This imbecile is addicted
to gambling, you see.

107
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:36,357
He's racked up such a
massive debt that it's

108
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:38,197
made him lose sight of
everything but greed.

109
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:39,357
I don't care about him.

110
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:41,017
I want to know why you're talking.

111
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,197
I've had about enough of you
running your mouth, Lucci.

112
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:47,240
This'll shut you up. Rope. Action!

113
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:49,820
Kukalukaku!

114
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:52,560
Blueno!

115
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:58,390
Punishment!

116
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,360
Oh no, he's serious, isn't he?

117
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:09,540
Hook, line and sinker!

118
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:15,390
Is he okay?

119
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:17,420
Well, this is nothing new.

120
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:23,040
Whoa, check it out!

121
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:27,559
I thought he landed on his head,
but he stopped himself with his arm.

122
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:29,650
Wow!

123
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:32,957
Not only that, but he dug
his hand into the ground.

124
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:35,922
What, does he have fingers
of steel or something?

125
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:38,217
These guys are just shipwrights.

126
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:40,947
What do they need all
these crazy powers for?

127
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,494
Hey, you with the goggles on your head!

128
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:48,697
Listen, man, think
about this for a second.

129
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:51,657
That guy wasn't the one
saying all that mean stuff.

130
00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:53,160
It was the pigeon!

131
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,959
The bird could say whatever it was.

132
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:04,077
Actions speak louder than words,
and Lucy's the one who hit me.

133
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:04,990
Yeah,

134
00:08:05,073 --> 00:08:09,017
but the pigeon made him hit you because
he's too much of a coward to do it himself.

135
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:10,140
Hey, pigeon!

136
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:13,393
Get down here and fight your own fights!

137
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:27,120
Oh you want some more I'll take
you on any time enough Paulie?

138
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:31,340
Please excuse us for all the fuss.

139
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:36,057
I'm Rob Luke I mean my name's
Huttery and this is Rob

140
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:39,357
Lucci we both welcome you to
at the Gallylaw's factory.

141
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:40,357
Coo-coo-doo-coo!

142
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:44,499
Hold on, the pigeon almost introduced
himself as that guy, didn't he?

143
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:45,680
Oh, cool!

144
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,800
This is the ventriloquism
act, isn't it? Really?

145
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:51,437
You were the coolest
trash-talking pigeon ever!

146
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:54,417
Even I couldn't tell it was you,
and I'm usually pretty sharp!

147
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:57,177
No, please, it's really
nothing special at all.

148
00:08:57,260 --> 00:08:58,090
Kaku!

149
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:03,079
He's just a weirdo who doesn't know
how to talk to people on his own.

150
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:07,757
Hold on a second, what does
that woman think she's doing?

151
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:08,670
Huh?

152
00:09:08,753 --> 00:09:09,583
What do you mean?

153
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:11,300
Have you no shame?

154
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:13,070
Huh?

155
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,357
Don't play dumb with me, you harlot!

156
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:17,240
I can see way too much leg!

157
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,657
Cover up and show a
little decency, will ya?

158
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:22,977
This duck is a man's workplace, you know?

159
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:24,001
Now, now, Polly.

160
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:25,520
Let's calm down.

161
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:32,380
You too, Khalifa!

162
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:35,537
How many times have I told you those
clothes of yours are not appropriate?

163
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:37,297
If you want to look professional,

164
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,717
you should wear pants or at
least ankle-length skirts!

165
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:40,797
This guy's got issues.

166
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,233
He's serious, isn't he?

167
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:46,200
Well, enough of that.

168
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:49,645
Let's open the gate and
start the tour already.

169
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,437
That's just about the
biggest door I've ever seen.

170
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:05,285
And, whoa, you have to
open that thing by hand?

171
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:08,839
Wait, those two can't open
that on their own, right?

172
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:12,720
Whoa, I guess they can.

173
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:14,070
Well,

174
00:10:14,153 --> 00:10:17,297
each doc only has five foremen,
and despite their peculiarities,

175
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:21,086
these two are both talented enough
to hold a position at Doc 1.

176
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:23,717
Kaku, who you met earlier,
is a foreman, too.

177
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:26,857
In a place like this, talent
is the only thing that matters.

178
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:29,397
So even if they're a bit
strange, we don't mind.

179
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:30,837
They are pretty weird, though.

180
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:32,155
Ha, we can go in now.

181
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:34,677
Remember to keep a
close eye on your money.

182
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:37,857
Not that I'm saying we have
thieves inside, mind you.

183
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:40,717
Yeah, I'm really sorry you
got dragged into that mess.

184
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,157
I should have known better.

185
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:43,070
Luffy, wait!

186
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:46,497
Do a better job of protecting
me next time around!

187
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:49,300
Okay, no problem. I got this. I'm worried.

188
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:50,680
I got this.

189
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:53,897
Doc One employs the strongest
workers of the Galley-La Company.

190
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:56,568
We take on our most
difficult requests here.

191
00:10:57,020 --> 00:10:59,460
Come on, let's take a look inside. Oh!

192
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:03,890
Whoa!

193
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:17,040
It's super huge!

194
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:21,897
I knew it was going to be awesome,
but it's even better up close!

195
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:23,260
No kidding! Yeah!

196
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:24,957
Ooh, look over there!

197
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:26,597
They're building a big galleon!

198
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:27,797
I wonder who it's for!

199
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,409
Wow, they've got a full
force working here.

200
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:32,220
Now, this way, if you will.

201
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:38,240
Whoa, you guys see that?
Iceberg's here. Hey!

202
00:11:38,740 --> 00:11:40,740
Look sharp for the boss, everyone.

203
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:41,737
Good morning, Mr.

204
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:42,650
Iceberg.

205
00:11:43,140 --> 00:11:44,669
Nice to see you here, sir.

206
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:48,217
Hey, boss, could you take a
look at the outer panels for me?

207
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:49,820
Sure, I'll stop by later.

208
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,077
Lovely day, you ate it, Mr.

209
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:52,990
Iceberg.

210
00:11:53,073 --> 00:11:55,397
Well, that depends on
how the project's going.

211
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:57,197
We're actually ahead of schedule, sir.

212
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:00,720
I guess Ice Pops is pretty
popular around here, huh?

213
00:12:01,420 --> 00:12:05,077
In a place where skill is everything,
it's only natural that he shines.

214
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:06,160
It's interesting.

215
00:12:06,340 --> 00:12:08,937
Although shipbuilding has
always been a thriving business,

216
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:12,937
before Iceberg's time, it was divided
between seven competing companies.

217
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:13,850
Regardless,

218
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:14,763
when he showed up,

219
00:12:14,846 --> 00:12:17,857
there was something about his talent
that united everyone in the city.

220
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:19,797
With so many workers flocking to him,

221
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:22,437
it wasn't long before he made
the seven companies into one.

222
00:12:22,520 --> 00:12:24,696
And five years ago, Galley-La was born.

223
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,077
His passion for shipbuilding
has never diminished,

224
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:31,689
and neither has the respect
that the craftsmen show for him.

225
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:35,517
The people here take pride in their
work so they don't bow down to anyone.

226
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:38,424
That goes for pirates and
marine officers alike.

227
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:41,552
That's the spirit this shipyard embodies.

228
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:45,297
Hey, buddy, I hear you're
a pretty good shipwright.

229
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:47,137
How would you feel about joining my crew?

230
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:48,097
Sound like fun?

231
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:50,157
What the hell are you
trying to pull here, kid?

232
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:51,557
You insolent little wretch!

233
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:53,617
Now, now, he was trying
to pay him a compliment.

234
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:55,177
He doesn't know any better.

235
00:12:55,260 --> 00:12:56,797
Does this mean you sailed all the way to

236
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:59,057
Water 7 without any
shipwrights on your crew?

237
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:01,077
Yeah, that's one of the
reasons why we're here.

238
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:04,717
We need to have our ship repaired, but
we're looking for a new crew member, too.

239
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,377
We certainly have plenty of
shipwrights to go around,

240
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:10,537
but I wonder if any of them would
be willing to join a pirate ship.

241
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:14,297
Well, if you can find someone who agrees
to go with you, I won't get in the way.

242
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:15,317
You really mean it?

243
00:13:15,400 --> 00:13:17,400
Wow, I see why people like you.

244
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:19,317
My offer still stands, you know.

245
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:20,897
Well, I probably shouldn't.

246
00:13:20,980 --> 00:13:22,880
I am the mayor. So what?

247
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:29,757
By the way, Kalifa mentioned earlier that
Nico Robin was a member of your crew,

248
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:30,670
is that correct?

249
00:13:30,753 --> 00:13:31,583
Sure is.

250
00:13:31,666 --> 00:13:34,225
She's nice to have around
since she's so smart.

251
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:37,670
What's up?

252
00:13:37,753 --> 00:13:39,857
There's not something
wrong with her, is there?

253
00:13:39,940 --> 00:13:40,780
Oh, no.

254
00:13:44,580 --> 00:13:46,992
I could watch them do this stuff all day.

255
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:50,820
Keep it steady. No need
to rush. We got this.

256
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:56,278
We gotta get a cannon like that to
put on the Mary, that'd be awesome.

257
00:13:56,660 --> 00:13:58,737
Whoa, it's so shiny and long!

258
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:00,657
A real Timmy Culvering cannon.

259
00:14:00,740 --> 00:14:03,020
Wow, they've really got everything.

260
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:06,862
I wonder what it feels
like to fire one of those.

261
00:14:07,580 --> 00:14:08,800
Here's our chance.

262
00:14:09,300 --> 00:14:10,917
Looks like it's made of copper.

263
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,920
And it's super expensive.

264
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:15,537
I wonder if they'd let me try it out?

265
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:16,977
Oh, wait a sec.

266
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:19,837
We've got 300 million
berries, I almost forgot.

267
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:22,417
We can afford at least
one of those, no problem.

268
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:23,618
Maybe two or three.

269
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:31,470
Hmm?

270
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:34,912
Did that guy with the nose leave already?

271
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:47,740
Huh? Kaku?

272
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:50,557
Where'd you run off to?

273
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:51,470
An assessment.

274
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:53,140
Wow, you are fast, huh?

275
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:54,637
I take it you checked the ship?

276
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:55,779
That I did, Missy.

277
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:57,417
Now, that's a coincidence.

278
00:14:57,500 --> 00:14:59,197
We were wondering where you were.

279
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:02,221
They told me while they're
here, how was the ship?

280
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:03,650
About that.

281
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:06,510
Loopy!

282
00:15:06,593 --> 00:15:07,423
Get down here!

283
00:15:11,740 --> 00:15:13,940
Get down here!

284
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:25,290
What?

285
00:15:25,373 --> 00:15:26,497
You better not be lying.

286
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:29,537
Because if this is a joke,
I'm sure as hell not laughing.

287
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,717
I wouldn't lie about something like this.

288
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:32,918
I'm a professional.

289
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:49,789
Something happen to Usopp?

290
00:15:50,420 --> 00:15:54,361
Well, he was looking at the cannons
over there a second ago, but...

291
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:58,060
Hey! You there!

292
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:00,540
These bags are yours, aren't they?

293
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:07,557
Usopp was supposed to be taking care
of those, but yeah, they're ours.

294
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:08,470
Thanks, buddy.

295
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:11,897
I can't believe that idiot
wandering off at a time like this.

296
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:14,537
And how can I leave our
precious money alone like that?

297
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:15,450
Your money?

298
00:16:15,533 --> 00:16:18,889
Yeah, those bags you found have
200 million berries in them.

299
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:20,900
You're funny, kid.

300
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:23,280
You wish. See ya.

301
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:25,033
Man, that's a good one.

302
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:29,277
So, how much do you think the
repairs are going to cost?

303
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:33,713
We've got plenty of money to spend,
and no price is too high for our Mary.

304
00:16:35,380 --> 00:16:37,877
And while you're at it, why
don't you make her stronger,

305
00:16:37,960 --> 00:16:41,901
faster, as tough as nails, and, of
course, decked out with cannons?

306
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:45,777
I'd like some pretty engravings or
some kind of decoration along the hull.

307
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:48,154
Also, can you guys renovate the inside?

308
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:49,240
Not so fast.

309
00:16:51,040 --> 00:16:55,805
That ship of yours, she's gone on some
really extraordinary journeys, hasn't she?

310
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,917
Yeah, you bet, we've done it all.

311
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,697
A while back we flew in the
sky, then fell back down.

312
00:17:01,780 --> 00:17:03,257
We climbed a big mountain, too.

313
00:17:03,340 --> 00:17:06,811
So, you've got to work hard to
fix her up extra good, okay?

314
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,706
Her battle scars are far
worse than you think.

315
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:14,037
Um, then you're saying it's going to
take a long time for you to fix her?

316
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:14,950
No, ma 'am.

317
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:17,372
It's not about the time.

318
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:19,306
It's not about skill, either.

319
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:23,460
I'm saying no one can
fix her. Not even us.

320
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:41,237
So that means the keel's
damaged huh that's

321
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:44,557
right it looks like it's
taken a real wallop.

322
00:17:44,640 --> 00:17:49,457
No way we we've gone
everywhere without any problems

323
00:17:49,540 --> 00:17:53,220
until now in theory even if
we did everything we could

324
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,652
the probability of your ship
lasting till the next island?

325
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:02,000
is zero.

326
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:15,840
Mary, tell me.

327
00:18:16,200 --> 00:18:17,620
Is what he said true?

328
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:19,900
You'll never sail again?

329
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:25,300
This sucks

330
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:29,877
I'm sorry Sanji I was so
focused on the books that I

331
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:32,637
didn't notice when she left
There's nothing you should have

332
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:35,917
to apologize for I just
assumed that the person I saw

333
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,471
walking with Robin had to have
been you and your human form

334
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:43,197
Which begs the question
who was that masked man

335
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:46,045
and how were they able
to disappear so suddenly

336
00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:49,800
Yeah even Robin's scent disappeared

337
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:57,237
I hope everything's all
right I feel uneasy do

338
00:18:57,320 --> 00:19:00,397
you need an examination
no that's not what I meant

339
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:03,957
It's what Aokiji said
his words keep repeating

340
00:19:04,040 --> 00:19:07,797
what did he say that
worries you it was all that

341
00:19:07,880 --> 00:19:10,597
Terrible stuff he said
about Robin's past and

342
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:13,577
then very soon we'd regret
her being on our crew

343
00:19:13,660 --> 00:19:16,917
Oh yeah right so do you
think what he said might

344
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,277
have had something to do
with her disappearance?

345
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:20,190
I don't know.

346
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:23,397
But I know we're getting
nowhere looking for her.

347
00:19:23,480 --> 00:19:25,598
Let's head back to the ship for now.

348
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:28,897
If nothing's wrong, she'll come
back on her own eventually.

349
00:19:28,980 --> 00:19:30,098
Yeah, you're right.

350
00:19:49,180 --> 00:19:50,010
What?

351
00:19:50,093 --> 00:19:52,360
You can't fix the going, Mary? Why not?

352
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:56,268
I thought all of you were supposed
to be awesome shipwrights.

353
00:19:57,300 --> 00:19:58,817
Don't worry about the money.

354
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:00,076
Look, we got plenty.

355
00:20:00,220 --> 00:20:01,985
It's not about the money, son.

356
00:20:02,460 --> 00:20:07,260
No matter how much you pay, that ship
can't be restored to how she used to be.

357
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:12,897
To be honest, I'm rather impressed you
were able to get that old girl this far,

358
00:20:12,980 --> 00:20:15,317
considering the condition
that she's currently in.

359
00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:16,577
What's that supposed to mean?

360
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:18,877
What exactly is going on with the Mary?

361
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:21,700
Do you know what the
keel is, immoral girl?

362
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,497
The thing at the bottom of the boat?

363
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:26,874
Yeah, it's the most important
piece of lumber on a ship.

364
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:30,140
It crosses from stem to
stern and provides support.

365
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:34,500
When you build a ship, you
start off by placing the keel.

366
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:37,366
Then you set the wood
for the stem, the stern,

367
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:40,071
the frame, frame brackets, and deck beams.

368
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:42,937
All of the pieces are
carefully assembled together,

369
00:20:43,020 --> 00:20:45,260
with the keel serving as the core.

370
00:20:46,700 --> 00:20:50,157
It's the very backbone framework
and life of every vessel

371
00:20:50,240 --> 00:20:53,937
constructed and when it's severely
damaged Then that's the end of her.

372
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:57,097
You can't just replace it
with a new one That'd be

373
00:20:57,180 --> 00:21:00,180
the same thing as building
a new ship from scratch.

374
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:05,477
That's the reason we or anyone
won't be able to fix her your ships?

375
00:21:05,560 --> 00:21:08,337
Just a bunch of woods stuck
together awaiting her end.

376
00:21:08,420 --> 00:21:10,720
Hey you don't say such horrible things

377
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:14,260
Horrible or not, it's the truth

378
00:21:16,180 --> 00:21:17,460
Okay, fine, then.

379
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:20,637
Just rebuild her from the
ground up like you said before.

380
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:22,249
Build another Going Merry.

381
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:23,890
Cuckoo!

382
00:21:23,973 --> 00:21:25,900
Sorry, not possible. Why not?

383
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:28,640
We could build you a very similar ship,

384
00:21:28,760 --> 00:21:32,157
but no one can build you one
that would be exactly the same.

385
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:34,957
Nowhere in the world are there
two completely identical ships.

386
00:21:35,040 --> 00:21:36,357
It simply can't be done.

387
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:37,417
What do you mean?

388
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:38,330
Why?

389
00:21:38,413 --> 00:21:42,154
Tell me, are there any trees in the
world that grow exactly the same way?

390
00:21:42,480 --> 00:21:45,280
A sailing ship is mostly
built out of lumber,

391
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:47,437
so the overall size and shape of a vessel?

392
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:49,537
is determined by the type of wood used.

393
00:21:49,620 --> 00:21:51,617
That's why an exact
duplicate of your ship?

394
00:21:51,700 --> 00:21:53,377
Can't be built by us or anyone.

395
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:55,578
even when using the same blueprints.

396
00:21:55,820 --> 00:21:58,757
If we made a ship like that
with those specifications,

397
00:21:58,840 --> 00:22:00,857
the ones who would
notice the difference and

398
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:02,877
feel that it's a
completely different ship?

399
00:22:02,960 --> 00:22:05,940
would be all of you guys! Cuckoo!

400
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:07,740
No way.

401
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:10,557
So you're saying it's
absolutely impossible

402
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:13,287
for us to continue
sailing on the Going Mary?

403
00:22:13,440 --> 00:22:14,580
Unfortunately, yes.

404
00:22:14,900 --> 00:22:17,429
You can either wait
around for her to sink,

405
00:22:17,920 --> 00:22:20,420
or you can dismantle her immediately.

406
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:23,260
A real Carabelle!

407
00:22:23,400 --> 00:22:24,680
What a cool ship!

408
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:26,077
Have you guys seen the front?

409
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:27,680
It's totally awesome!

410
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:31,200
Okay, it's all done!

411
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:35,020
Now the Going Mary has everything
she could possibly need!

412
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:39,117
Well, your ship is on her last legs.

413
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:41,777
Why don't you look at
this as an opportunity?

414
00:22:41,860 --> 00:22:44,917
You happen to be in the best place
in the world to buy a new ship.

415
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:45,997
You have money, right?

416
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:48,551
I understand that your ship was a caravel.

417
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:54,221
Sailing on the Grand Line in an old ship
like that would be difficult to begin with.

418
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:55,230
No way.

419
00:22:55,313 --> 00:22:57,400
We're not getting a new ship. Luffy.

420
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:00,157
The Mary is...

421
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:03,517
The going Mary is the ship
of the Straw Hat Pirates.

422
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:05,917
If she's repaired, then
she can still sail.

423
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:06,897
She'll be fine.

424
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:07,810
You'll see.

425
00:23:07,893 --> 00:23:09,560
She got us here today, didn't she?

426
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:12,317
And you tell us out of nowhere
she can't sail anymore?

427
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:14,580
I can't believe that. I won't.

428
00:23:14,740 --> 00:23:17,257
You guys are saying that
because you don't know her.

429
00:23:17,340 --> 00:23:19,840
You have no idea how strong she is.

430
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:23,936
Then your plan is to keep
sailing her until she sinks?

431
00:23:26,960 --> 00:23:29,725
So, this is the decision
of the ship's captain?

432
00:23:32,260 --> 00:23:34,200
This discussion is over for now.

433
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:35,577
Just think about it.

434
00:23:35,660 --> 00:23:37,857
If you decide to buy a ship, let me know.

435
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:39,058
My people can help.

436
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:43,317
If you're able to spend $300 million, we
can build a state-of-the-art ship for you.

437
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:44,230
Khalifa?

438
00:23:45,340 --> 00:23:47,417
Here, if you'd look this over, please.

439
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:49,977
It's a catalog of our
new and our used models.

440
00:23:50,060 --> 00:23:51,707
Use it as a price reference.

441
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:53,020
Hey, Iceberg!

442
00:23:54,240 --> 00:23:56,977
There's someone at the front
gate who wants to see you.

443
00:23:57,060 --> 00:23:59,897
It looks like those world
government officials again.

444
00:23:59,980 --> 00:24:02,215
Should I tell them you're unavailable?

445
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:05,140
Well, yes, please. Say I'm gone.

446
00:24:05,660 --> 00:24:08,960
By the way, Lulu, you've
got quite a case of bedhead.

447
00:24:09,220 --> 00:24:11,440
Oops, how embarrassing. My apologies.

448
00:24:11,820 --> 00:24:14,640
Anyway, I'll ask them
to leave. Uh, thanks.

449
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:15,637
I appreciate it.

450
00:24:15,720 --> 00:24:18,220
Hold it right there, Iceberg. Hmm?

451
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:22,220
Looks like you're here after all.

452
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:24,380
Hey, you two need to hide.

453
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:25,757
Government officials are here.

454
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:26,980
What? Uh, okay.

455
00:24:32,700 --> 00:24:34,797
Well, hello again, Corgi.

456
00:24:34,880 --> 00:24:36,939
As you can see, I'm not here today.

457
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:39,240
Yes, you are. Come now.

458
00:24:39,500 --> 00:24:41,817
I've come all the way
out here by Sea Train.

459
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:42,917
Don't turn me away.

460
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:43,830
It's impolite.

461
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:54,320
For some reason, I detect
the scent of pirates.

462
00:24:55,660 --> 00:24:56,490
Whatever.

463
00:24:56,680 --> 00:24:58,577
In any case, I want to talk to you.

464
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:00,420
I don't like you. Go away.

465
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:02,960
Are you a child? Nuh-uh.

466
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:04,395
I'm totally an adult.

467
00:25:05,780 --> 00:25:08,620
Hmm, then start acting like one.

468
00:25:08,780 --> 00:25:11,837
There's something of special
importance I need to discuss with you.

469
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:14,449
I would appreciate it
if you'd hear me out.

470
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:17,880
Come, let's talk this over in private.

471
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:23,447
Those government guys
are so damn persistent.

472
00:25:23,540 --> 00:25:26,117
What the hell do they
want with Iceberg anyway?

473
00:25:26,200 --> 00:25:27,917
Look, it has nothing to do with us.

474
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,471
Probably political stuff.

475
00:25:30,020 --> 00:25:32,580
Should I beat them up? I wouldn't.

476
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:35,231
I mean, they're government guys after all.

477
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:40,550
What?

478
00:25:40,660 --> 00:25:41,580
What's wrong?

479
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:46,089
Something seemed weird about
these bags when I grabbed them.

480
00:25:48,420 --> 00:25:49,840
They're really light.

481
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:51,550
Huh?

482
00:25:51,633 --> 00:25:52,463
What do you mean?

483
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:55,240
Oh, come on, stop kidding around.

484
00:25:55,580 --> 00:25:58,404
How could they be with
all of that money inside?

485
00:26:03,720 --> 00:26:04,920
Hey, who's that?

486
00:26:05,160 --> 00:26:06,689
What's with the screaming?

487
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:10,177
Idiots, what the hell are you...

488
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:14,280
Hey, lady, if you sit like
that, everyone can see your...

489
00:26:16,080 --> 00:26:17,097
Don't tell me.

490
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:18,340
There's no way.

491
00:26:20,060 --> 00:26:22,080
I don't want to look.

492
00:26:25,040 --> 00:26:26,077
200 million!

493
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:27,200
Gone?

494
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:28,820
It's really gone!

495
00:26:29,180 --> 00:26:32,004
Wait, look at these bags,
they're not even ours!

496
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:37,020
Hey, hey, keep it down! What's wrong?

497
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:40,280
Our money, our money's gone.

498
00:26:40,780 --> 00:26:42,820
Finished, gone, disappeared.

499
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:44,230
What?

500
00:26:44,313 --> 00:26:47,200
Polly, was it you again? Huh? No! Honest?

501
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:49,340
Why would I lie? You mean it?

502
00:26:49,820 --> 00:26:51,100
They're so noisy.

503
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:52,957
Hey, Kaku, question for you.

504
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:55,537
Why were you hanging out with
the Franky family earlier?

505
00:26:55,620 --> 00:26:57,097
What are you talking about?

506
00:26:57,180 --> 00:27:00,837
